# Copy to .env for local development: `cp .env.example .env`
# Both workspaces load it via `node --env-file-if-exists=../.env` (see package.json scripts).
#
# In production do NOT ship this file — inject these as real environment variables.
# The backend refuses to boot if the starred (*) ones are missing or still at defaults.

# ---------- required in production ----------
DATABASE_URL=postgres://qrreward:qrreward@localhost:5436/qrreward
# Managed Postgres: append ?sslmode=require (pg reads it straight from the URL).
# Used by the app and by the Prisma CLI (backend/prisma.config.ts loads this file).

# * Public origin of the API. Encoded into every QR as ${BASE_URL}/r/{code}.
#   Getting this wrong after codes are printed means a reprint — there is no fix in software.
BASE_URL=http://localhost:4000

# * Browser origin(s) allowed by CORS, comma-separated. The first is canonical and is
#   used for scan redirects. Must be https in production.
FRONTEND_URL=http://localhost:3000

# * Signs sessions and scan tokens. Generate with: openssl rand -hex 32
#   Booting production with the value below is refused outright — it is a full auth bypass.
JWT_SECRET=dev-secret-change-me

# * Super admin, seeded on first boot only. Never created through signup.
#   In production the password must be 12+ chars and is NOT reset on redeploy.
ADMIN_EMAIL=admin@qrreward.local
ADMIN_PASSWORD=admin12345

# ---------- optional ----------
# Both default to ON outside production and OFF in production.
#
# Unauthenticated Swagger UI at /docs. It enumerates every route, body and auth scheme, so
# publishing it in production is free reconnaissance. Set true only if you mean it.
# ENABLE_DOCS=false
#
# `POST /v1/campaigns/:id/fund` credits a campaign budget with NO payment behind it — a
# promoter can mint the budget that pays publishers real fees. Keep it off until checkout is
# wired up; admins can still fund deliberately via the audited campaigns/:id/adjust route.
# ALLOW_SELF_FUNDING=false

# ---------- the business model ----------
# The platform's take rate in basis points: 1000 = 10% of every payout, retained as
# `platform:fees` in the same double-entry transaction that pays the publisher. Without this
# the ledger moves the promoter's money to the publisher at 1:1 and the platform earns nothing.
#
# Snapshotted onto each partnership at creation, never read live at payout time — so changing
# this reprices only FUTURE partnerships. Existing ones are repriced explicitly by an admin
# via PATCH /v1/admin/partnerships/:id { platform_fee_bps }, which is audited.
# PLATFORM_FEE_BPS=1000
#
# How long earned fees stay unwithdrawable. This is the clawback window that makes fraud
# review a control rather than a suggestion: every accepted risk in the threat model (self-
# scan, promoter/publisher collusion, a disputed attribution) is bounded by "the review runs
# before real money leaves". A publisher sees `earnings` immediately and `withdrawable` only
# after this many days. 0 disables the holdback — demo stacks only.
# SETTLEMENT_DELAY_DAYS=14
#
# HMAC-SHA256 secret shared with the payment provider's webhook adapter, over the RAW request
# body. Without it POST /v1/payments/webhook answers 404 and money-in is impossible — which is
# the correct failure mode: an unsigned funding webhook mints budgets for whoever finds the URL.
# Minimum 16 chars in production. Generate with: openssl rand -hex 32
# PAYMENT_WEBHOOK_SECRET=
#
# Whether a publisher signup is usable immediately. Off in production: a publisher RECEIVES
# money, so an admin approves it (PATCH /v1/admin/orgs/:id { approved: true }) before it
# appears in the directory or can enter a partnership. Promoters self-gate with their budget
# and are always approved.
# AUTO_APPROVE_PUBLISHERS=false
#
# Slack-compatible webhook for operational alerts, POSTed as { text }. Alerts always reach the
# structured log at level=error; this pushes them somewhere a human is actually looking.
# Fires on: ledger drift, an unbalanced book, and a campaign budget about to run dry.
# ALERT_WEBHOOK_URL=

# ---------- attribution tuning ----------
# How long a scan stays matchable, per path. Both are measured scan → FIRST OPEN, not
# scan → signup: an install lands minutes after a scan, a signup can land days later, and
# one window cannot honestly cover both. That is what the installs table separates.
# REFERRER_WINDOW_DAYS=30
# FINGERPRINT_WINDOW_MIN=60
#
# How long a matched install stays convertible into a paid signup — the second leg.
# SIGNUP_WINDOW_DAYS=30
#
# The accept/reject line for a probabilistic match, 0–100.
#   hashed IP + platform  55  (the filter, never the evidence)
#   + timezone           +10
#   + locale             +10
#   + screen geometry    +25  (the only signal that splits a country by handset)
# The default of 70 therefore REFUSES a bare IP + platform match. An IP is a postcode —
# carrier-grade NAT, cafe wifi, an airport, a corporate VPN — and paying on one attributes
# strangers to each other. Raise toward 80 to demand the screen match. Minimum accepted: 60.
# MIN_CONFIDENCE=70
#
# How far back the repeat-device check looks, on the fingerprint path only. The device hash
# is a device *shape*: identical handsets on one network with the same locale collide, so a
# blocked honest install is a real support ticket. 0 disables it. Consider that in high-NAT
# markets; consider raising it where install farming is the bigger cost.
# DEVICE_DEDUPE_DAYS=7

# PORT=4000
# DB_POOL_MAX=10
# `prisma migrate dev` replays migrations into a throwaway database to detect drift, and
# creates one itself by default. Managed Postgres usually forbids CREATE DATABASE — point
# this at a second empty database there instead. Never at the real one; it gets reset.
# SHADOW_DATABASE_URL=postgres://user:pw@host/qrreward_shadow
# NODE_ENV=production
# Behind a load balancer, req.ip must be the real client or per-IP rate limits collapse
# into one bucket. See https://expressjs.com/en/guide/behind-proxies.html
# Use the hop count or the proxy subnet. `true` is refused at boot: it trusts
# X-Forwarded-For from ANY client, so one attacker becomes unlimited distinct IPs and
# every per-IP control (login throttle, scan limit, iOS fingerprint) stops working.
# TRUST_PROXY=1

# ---------- running more than one instance ----------
# Where the rate limiters count. Unset means "in this process", which is correct for exactly
# one instance and is how the stack runs locally.
#
# This is the setting that gates horizontal scale, and it is a security setting rather than a
# performance one: every per-IP control here (login throttling, the signup and scan ceilings,
# the partner API budget, the global floor) is counted per process, so N replicas without a
# shared store means N x every limit — twice the login attempts before anyone is throttled.
# Set this before adding a second instance.
#
# A Redis outage degrades to the per-instance window and logs `ratelimit.redis_unavailable`;
# it never fails open and never takes the API down with it.
# REDIS_URL=redis://localhost:6379

# Bearer token for GET /metrics (Prometheus text format). Without it the endpoint 404s, which
# is the production default: the series include the attribution refusal rates, and those
# describe this platform's payout behaviour to anyone who asks. Minimum 16 chars in production.
#
# The series worth alerting on is `attribution_decisions_total{reason=...}`. A publisher whose
# store target is misconfigured produces no errors and writes no rows — every install just
# looks organic — so a `no_match` rate climbing toward 100% is the only symptom there is.
# METRICS_TOKEN=

# ---------- development only ----------
# These demo tenants are re-seeded on every boot and are SKIPPED entirely when
# NODE_ENV=production. Sign in with them at http://localhost:3000/login
PROMOTER_NAME=Air Dhaka
PROMOTER_EMAIL=promoter@demo.com
PROMOTER_PASSWORD=password123

PUBLISHER_NAME=DramaBox
PUBLISHER_EMAIL=publisher@demo.com
PUBLISHER_PASSWORD=password123
PUBLISHER_LANDING_URL=http://localhost:3000/publisher-sim
PUBLISHER_API_KEY=pk_devdevdevdevdevdevdevdevdevdevdevdevdevdevdevdev

# Baked into the frontend bundle at BUILD time, not read at runtime.
NEXT_PUBLIC_API_URL=http://localhost:4000
# Prefilled into the login form
NEXT_PUBLIC_DEMO_EMAIL=promoter@demo.com
NEXT_PUBLIC_DEMO_PASSWORD=password123
